  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to combine variance reduction with perturbation techniques to escape local optima in the Erds minimum overlap problem. The algorithm phases involve:\n- **Annealed perturbations**: Starting from parent solutions, apply structured patterns that reduce maximum overlap\n- **Delta-correlated adjustments**: Random modifications that maintain integral constraints and capture periodic patterns\n- **Resolution-aware optimization**: Progressively increasing grid resolution to reveal finer structures\n- **Coordinate-wise exploration**: Targeted updates that address specific periodic components\n\nThe key innovations are:\n- Using deterministic pattern insertion (sawtooth) alongside randomized mutations\n- Adaptive delta-correlation initialization that preserves integral constraints\n- Constrained optimization via successive clipping and integral rebalancing\n- Progressive resolution optimization with smooth score approximation\n\nThe method systematically explores the function space by:\n- Global exploration (simulated annealing)\n- Local refinement (coordinate descent)\n- Pattern insertion\n- Randomized mutations\n- Progressive resolution increases\n\nThese phases work together to systematically lower the C5 bound, leveraging both structure-aware and randomized searches to escape local minima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe problem is defined by:\n1. Minimize C5 = argmax_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n2. Subject to: \u222bh(x)dx = 1, 0\u2264h(x)\u22641\n3. Discretization: h_i \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h_i = n/2\n4. Performance measure: max_k(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")[k]) * (2/n)\n\nThe algorithm maintains:\n- Current solution vector h \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u1d5b\n- Current grid resolution n\u1d5b\n- Global best solution h_best with lowest C5\n- Simulated annealing configuration parameters\n- Coordinate-wise search points\n\nThe perturbation mechanisms include:\nSawtooth pattern insertion: \n   h_i = target + (i - offset) * s \n   where s = sin(phase factor) * amplitude\n\nCoordinate descent updates:\n   Adjust h_i to reduce extreme overlap at k = \u00b1p (p integer)\n\nConstraint handling:\n   Preserve integral via: \n      h_projected = np.clip(np.exp(delta * alpha), 0, 1) * N/(2 * np.sum(...))\n\n   This ensures \u2211h + \u0394 = N/2 through discrete adjustment\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm executes in distinct phases:\n\nPhase 0 Initialization\n- Load parent solution (n0=384) if available\n- Set initial grid and delta storage parameters\n- Apply initial delta-correlated perturbation\n- Project to satisfy [0,1] and integrality constraints\n\nPhase 1 Simulated Annealing\n- Initialize temperature T0 = 100\n- Implement Metropolis criterion with logarithmic cooling\n- Generate newborn sequence at each stage to prevent symmetry trapping\n- Update global best solution\n\nPhase 2 Local Refinement\n- Transition to coordinate-wise update using integral projection\n- Apply sawtooth perturbation with adaptive phase\n- Compute gradient via finite-differences with clipping targets\n\nPhase 3 Progressive Resolution\n- Double grid resolution (n *= 2)\n- Maintain key features via interpolation with integral matching\n- Compute adjusted temperature based on grid scaling\n- Execute annealed optimization at new resolution\n- Project newborn patterns to maintain structure\n\nPhase 4 Performance Evaluation\n- At each resolution, evaluate all unconstrained components\n- Maintain running maximum of minimal overlap\n- Re-evaluate with clipping-consistent optimization\n- Track progress across grid resolutions\n\nPhase 5 Final Adjustment\n- Perform adaptive descent on large-grained structure\n- Implement resolution-insensitive coordinate smoothing\n- Project final solution to satisfy all conditions\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nThe default search configuration includes:\n\nKey parameters:\n- Base resolution: 384\n- Base grid: initial_h_values\n- Temperature schedule: Geometric cooling from 100 to 0.001\n- Mutation amplitude: 0.1\n- Pattern persistence: 0.8\n- Clipping window: [0, 1]\n- Integration value: 1.0\n- Window expansion: 5% per annealing step\n\nAdvanced variations include:\n- Variable bottom persistence [0.6, 0.9]\n- Tunable mutation schemes (constant/adaptive)\n- Multiple pattern phases (phase count 5 to 15)\n- Bounded coordinate descent depth (depth 10 to 100)\n\nThe method alternates between structured pattern insertion (phase 1) and local random mutation (phase 2), with progressive resolution being controlled by a2 parameter.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe algorithm's computational profile includes:\n\n- Operation complexity: O(n\u00b2) per resolution\n- Memory needs: O(n) storage per grid\n- Time estimates:\n  - SA: O(n) evaluations per temperature cycle\n  - CD: O(k) updates per coordinate\n  - Pattern insertion: O(n) operations\n  - Resolution change: O(n) interpolation\n\nOrchestration:\n- Allocate 2/3 budget to multi-resolution search\n- 1/3 budget to final refinement\n- Break on time limits using total_budget parameter\n- Check for convergence on each resolution\n- Maintain fallback at each grid level  \n\nThe algorithm cautiously scales to grid doubling without timing out.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal validation ensures:\n- Stickiness: All values clipped to [0,1]\n- Integrality: sum(h) within 1e-10 of target\n- Finite score: C5 computed by np.correlate (full mode)\n- Performance returns: (h_values, C5_bound, n_points)\n-Lower is better (minimizing overlap)\n\nImplementation uses:\n- Clipping and projection utilities\n- Metropolis criterion\n- Percentage-based cooling\n- Guaranteed coordinate adjustment\n- Gradient-consistent smoothing\n\nTesting requires objective consistency and timing control.\n\nThe solution maintains:\n- Forward compatibility via grid doubling\n- Curveball resilience via perturbation scheme\n- Null fallback via initial solution\n- Score consistency via finite-difference evaluation",
